      "text": "We can represent the line with two points: \\[\n\\textbf{v} = t \\cdot \\textbf{d} + \\textbf{p},\n\\] where $\\textbf{v}$ is the vector on the line, $t$ is the parameter, $\\textbf{d}$ is the direction vector of the line, and $\\textbf{p}$ is the position vector.  Using $\\textbf{v} = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 4 \\end{pmatrix}$ and $\\textbf{p} = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 4 \\end{pmatrix},$ we have \\[\n\\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 4 \\end{pmatrix} = t \\cdot \\textbf{d} + \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 4 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\] In other words, $\\textbf{p}$ does not change with $t$ and $\\textbf{d}$ is the direction vector of the line.  The point $\\begin{pmatrix} 3 \\\\ -4 \\end{pmatrix}$ is not on the line of direction vector $d,$ but its vector from $\\textbf{p}$ is on the line, and thus we have \\[\n\\begin{pmatrix} 3 + 1 \\\\ -4 - 4 \\end{pmatrix} = t \\cdot \\textbf{d} + \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 4 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\] Near some moment in time, $t = 3,$ from which it follows that \\[\n\\textbf{p} = \\begin{pmatrix} 3 + 1 \\\\ -4 - 4 \\end{pmatrix} - 3\\cdot \\textbf{d}\n= \\begin{pmatrix} 3 \\\\ -4 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\] It follows that $\\textbf{d} = -\\vec{i} - \\vec{j}$ and $\\textbf{p} = \\begin{pmatrix} -3 \\\\ 4 \\end{pmatrix}.$ \n\nNow, the vector of the unknown point on the line is \\begin{align*}\n\\boxed{t \\cdot \\textbf{d} + \\begin{pmatrix} -3 \\\\ 4 \\end{pmatrix}} &= t \\cdot (\\begin{pmatrix} -1 \\\\ -1 \\end{pmatrix}) + \\begin{pmatrix} -3 \\\\ 4 \\end{pmatrix},\n\\end{align*} and evaluating at $t = -7,$ we find that \\[\n\\boxed{\\begin{pmatrix} -3 \\\\ 4 \\end{pmatrix}} - 7\\cdot (\\begin{pmatrix} -1 \\\\ -1 \\end{pmatrix}) = \\boxed{\\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\\\ 11 \\end{pmatrix}}.\n\\] We do not need to assume the direction vector or the position vector is non-zero.",
