    {
      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To solve for the vector on the line at \\( t = -7 \\), we need to first determine the parametric equation of the line. The line is parameterized by a parameter \\( t \\), and we know two points on the line: when \\( t = 2 \\), the vector is \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 4 \\end{pmatrix}\\); when \\( t = 3 \\), the vector is \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 3 \\\\ -4 \\end{pmatrix}\\).\n\nThe general parametric form of a line passing through two points \\(\\begin{pmatrix} x_1 \\\\ y_1 \\end{pmatrix}\\) and \\(\\begin{pmatrix} x_2 \\\\ y_2 \\end{pmatrix}\\) is given by:\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix} x \\\\ y \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} x_1 \\\\ y_1 \\end{pmatrix} + t \\begin{pmatrix} x_2 - x_1 \\\\ y_2 - y_1 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\nFirst, we calculate the direction vector:\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix} 3 \\\\ -4 \\end{pmatrix} - \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 4 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 3 - 1 \\\\ -4 - 4 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\\\ -8 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\nThus, the parametric form of the line can be written as:\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix} x \\\\ y \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 4 \\end{pmatrix} + t \\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\\\ -8 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\nWe need to find the vector at \\( t = -7 \\). The vector equation becomes:\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix} x \\\\ y \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 4 \\end{pmatrix} + (-7) \\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\\\ -8 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\nCalculating \\( t \\) for \\( t = -7 \\):\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix} x \\\\ y \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 4 \\end{pmatrix} + (-7) \\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\\\ -8 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 4 \\end{pmatrix} + \\begin{pmatrix} (-7)(2) \\\\ (-7)(-8) \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\nSimplifying this, we get:\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix} x \\\\ y \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 4 \\end{pmatrix} + \\begin{pmatrix} -14 \\\\ 56 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 - 14 \\\\ 4 + 56 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} -13 \\\\ 60 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the vector on the line at \\( t = -7 \\) is:\n\\boxed{\\begin{pmatrix} -13 \\\\ 60 \\end{pmatrix}}",
